{
  "schema": "ld-s10y-image/figure-spec@2",
  "id": "fig-147",
  "mode": "deterministic",
  "description": "Construct CD using D, the intersection of the focal plane and the secondary axis through curvature center O parallel to AC. A paraxial schematic with explicitly enlarged vertical scale, not an exact-angle drawing.",
  "source": {
    "image": {
      "path": "ssot-resources/soviet10year-textbooks/artifacts/10p/figures/fig-147.png",
      "sha256": "24ccc8527ad760dd8b62aef14492a555a4f64eb0b4b4eab8f2641220a11c17a0"
    },
    "authoritativeText": [
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s2-n71",
        "text": "If the position of an object relative to a spherical mirror is known, a ray construction can locate its **image**.\nFirst construct the image of an individual point, using a ruler and compass to determine the reflected ray paths.\nThe focusing constructions below use the paraxial approximation."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s2-n71",
        "text": "To construct the reflected path of the incident ray $AC$, draw a secondary optical axis parallel to $AC$ (Figure 147).\nThis secondary axis meets the focal plane at $D$.\nThe line through the point of incidence $C$ and $D$ gives the reflected ray path."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s2-n71",
        "text": "The construction follows from the fact that paraxial rays parallel to the same secondary axis meet at one point in the focal plane after reflection.\nFollowing just one of these rays is enough to locate that point.\nChoose the ray through the center of curvature $O$, along the secondary axis itself.\n\nThis ray retraces its straight path after reflection, so its reflected path also passes through the unknown point $D$.\nThus $D$ lies in both the focal plane and the secondary axis: it is their intersection."
      }
    ],
    "inventory": [
